# DTF tier manifest: sso-login-browser-binding  (artifact-keeper#5486)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single + sso=oidc (the oidc-group-sync mock IdP on
# 172.28.<slot>.0/24, profiles/sso.oidc.yml). SAML needs no IdP container: the
# assertions are signed by the sso tier's dtf-saml-xsw-probe with a key it
# generates at run time, whose certificate is registered as the provider's.
#
# THE BUG: an SSO sign-in was not bound to the browser that started it. The
# OIDC `state`, the SAML AuthnRequest id and the exchange code handed to the
# web UI were bearer values, so a sign-in could be completed in another browser
# than the one that started it, which then held that account's session.
#
# THE FIX (1.12.0): the login start sets a per-login cookie
#   ak_sso_bind_<16 hex>=<64 hex>; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/api/v1/auth/sso; Max-Age=600
# that the OIDC callback, the SAML completion step (GET /auth/sso/complete,
# which the ACS now 303s to without setting a session) and POST
# /auth/sso/exchange require. A refusal is 401 AUTH_ERROR and consumes nothing.
# `?client=cli` on the login URL starts a command-line sign-in: the callback /
# ACS needs no browser cookie, sets no cookie, and shows the exchange code as
# text/plain; /exchange redeems it only with the login response's cookie.
#
# PRIMITIVES
#   * Each browser is its own curl cookie jar (-b/-c). The IdP step (the mock's
#     /authorize, or the probe's signed Response) never carries a jar.
#   * "Set a session" means an ak_access_token Set-Cookie on the response.
#
# GATES (fixed image GREEN; pre-fix main RED on every FIX case)
#   CONTROL (both images):
#     - an OIDC login started, called back and exchanged in ONE jar completes,
#       and the exchanged token works on /auth/me as the IdP's user;
#     - a signed SAML response for a login started in one jar completes in that
#       jar, in either shape: ACS 307 /callback with the session cookies
#       (pre-fix), or ACS 303 /auth/sso/complete -> 307 /callback with them.
#   FIX:
#     - the OIDC login response sets the binding cookie (name, value, HttpOnly,
#       SameSite=Lax, Path, Max-Age=600); an unknown `client` value is a 400;
#     - an OIDC callback for a login started in jar A, delivered in jar B (which
#       holds the cookie of a sign-in of its own) -> 401 AUTH_ERROR "This sign-in
#       was not started in this browser", no Set-Cookie; the same callback then
#       completes in jar A (state not consumed). The same on the per-provider
#       callback path /oidc/{id}/callback with a jar B holding no cookie;
#     - an exchange code from a callback completed in jar A, POSTed from jar B
#       -> 401; then from jar A -> 200, the token works, and the binding cookie
#       is expired (Max-Age=0);
#     - a signed SAML response for a login started in jar A, POSTed to the ACS
#       from jar B -> 303 to /auth/sso/complete, no Set-Cookie; /complete in
#       jar B -> 401, no Set-Cookie; /complete in jar A -> 307 /callback with
#       the session cookies; the code redeems in jar A;
#     - OIDC `?client=cli`: state starts `cli-`; the callback in a jar without
#       the cookie -> 200 text/plain (no-store) showing a 64-hex code, no
#       Set-Cookie; /exchange without the cookie -> 401, with the login
#       response's cookie -> 200 and the token works;
#     - SAML `?client=cli`: AuthnRequest id starts `_cli-`; the ACS -> 200
#       text/plain with the code, no Set-Cookie; /exchange with the login
#       response's cookie -> 200.
#
# NOT COVERED: the web UI's /callback page (no frontend in this topology), and
# a real browser's SameSite enforcement (curl sends a jar's cookies on any
# request; the jars stand for what a browser would send).
#
# The SAML cases need the sso tier's signer: DTF_SAML_XSW_PROBE, or the
# prebuilt harness/tiers/sso/probe/target/release/dtf-saml-xsw-probe that the
# release gate's build-dtf-probes job provides.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: many SSO calls back to back from one client.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem sso.oidc"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
